 a thing like a i  b i this is perfectly equal  to a j b j  this is perfectly equal to a p  b p and so on and so forth  you keep on writing as long as you are  repeating the index at the same places  so that's what i  mean the letter i has been replaced by  letter j the letter i has been replaced  by the letter p  it does not make any difference so the  the the moment you see that something is  repeated it is just a dummy index  and its only job is to denote the  summation  okay as uh as noted down in point number  two  okay another very important  rule uh so we'll continue on the next  page is  is that an index  repeated more than once  an index repeated more than once  is  is meaningless  this is very important to note so  something like  uh a i b i c  i okay this is just wrong don't write  don't ever ever write like this  and we'll see uh immediately an  application of this particular rule  in our subsequent discussion so never  ever write  an index mode so never ever repeat an  index more than once  so had it been repeated only once so a  ibi that is fine  but repeated more than once so written  three times it doesn't make any sense  okay and finally  the the number  of free indices  the total number of free indices that  are present in a particular term  denotes the order of that term denotes  the  the order of the tensor of that term  so when we say something like a i  so you see here the i is not repeated  any index which is not repeated is  referred to as a  free index okay so free indices  basically means an  index which is not repeated so uh so  let's say  when we go back to this example  this i here is repeated so i is not a  free index in this particular case but  when we are writing  only one i here there is nothing  repeated here it is a free index  and the number of free indices in this  entire term is one  so the order of the tensor is  tensor of order one  okay which we know uh  is a vector now  at this point i would like to point out  that  uh so s